UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 2, Lesson 1: How our community has changed', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Historians compare the past and present to find what has changed and what has stayed the same (continuity). Both can be shown by evidence such as photographs, maps and the memories of older people.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nSort into change or continuity: trams replaced by buses; the post office is on the same corner; people still meet at the park on Saturdays.\n\nFEEDBACK\nTrams replaced by buses is change. The post office on the same corner and people meeting at the park are continuity.\n\nWATCH FOR\nSaying that everything has changed, or that nothing has, ignores the evidence that both happen.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a place in your community. Use two sources such as a photo and an older person. Complete a Then / Now / Change / Continuity table with five rows. Suggest one cause of one change.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"HSIE","learning_intention":"Historians compare the past and present to find what has changed and what has stayed the same (continuity). Both can be shown by evidence such as photographs, maps and the memories of older people.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Investigate","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["HS2-HIS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a place in your community. Use two sources such as a photo and an older person. Complete a Then / Now / Change / Continuity table with five rows. Suggest one cause of one change. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: Example: an old photo of Elm Street shows a dirt road, a corner shop and a horse and cart. A recent photo shows a sealed road, a supermarket and many cars. Change: road surface, shops, transport. Continuity: the creek still crosses at the same bend and the school sits on the same hill.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Saying that everything has changed, or that nothing has, ignores the evidence that both happen. Correction: Trams replaced by buses is change. The post office on the same corner and people meeting at the park are continuity.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Saying that everything has changed, or that nothing has, ignores the evidence that both happen. Keep sample answers out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Historians compare the past and present to find what has changed and what has stayed the same (continuity). Both can be shown by evidence such as photographs, maps and the memories of older people. The other choices avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Historians compare the past and present to find what has changed and what has stayed the same (continuity). Both can be shown by evidence such as photographs, maps and the memories of older people.","Saying that everything has changed, or that nothing has, ignores the evidence that both happen.","Ignore the source and invent a more exciting story.","Copy the model places and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taugh